ical and Military  leadership to justice for committed for  committing the crime of aggression  against Ukraine I thank you  I thank the representative of Estonia  for their statement  I now give the floor to his Excellency  Mr Olive Scott  head of the delegation of the European  Union to the United Nations  thank you very much Mr President for  convening us and thank you for the  opportunity to address the council this  afternoon and thank you to Rosemary  DeCarlo for a very comprehensive  briefing  I have the honor to speak on behalf of  the European Union and its member states  and I would like to highlight the  following three points one the  importance of upholding International  humanitarian law secondly on  accountability for crimes committed in  Ukraine and thirdly on the global  consequences of Russia's war of  aggression first Mr President on  International humanitarian law during  the past weeks Russia has stepped up its  targeted strikes against Ukraine  civilians and civilian infrastructure  yesterday saw the largest and gravest  missile attacks since the start of the  invasion  we express our condolences to the  families of the victims in both Ukraine  and in Poland  by deliberately disrupting energy and  water supply Russia is trying to  paralyze Ukraine and saw despair within  the population as winter approaches  these unacceptable actions targeting  civilians and civilian infrastructure by  Russia are unlawful and they constitute  war crimes  the IMF estimates that Ukraine may need  four billion dollars a month just to  keep the lights on and the Taps running  we cannot leave the Ukrainian people in  such a situation the EU is there for  undertaking urgent Rehabilitation  measures to prepare for winter and  repair the energy infrastructure bombed  by Russia  we are also working on a package to  ensure Ukraine's macroeconomic stability  maintaining essential public services  and rebuild critical infrastructure  I also reiterate the need for full  hum